```

The test method for each ArrayList method consists of the following:

- For methods, assumption is done that the provided parameters will be in line with the preconditions of each method, represented by `PexAssumeUnderTest`.
- Older state of the ArrayList is captured and then method being tested is invoked.
- Newer state of the ArrayList is captured, which is then compared to the expected new state.
- If the expected new state is matching the real one, then the test passes, implying the method behaves as expected.

#### Generated Tests

The generated tests have a structure reflecting the structure of the original ArrayList class, and are named as per their functionality. For instance, the tests `PUT_AddContract`, `PUT_RemoveContract` etc are test cases written to validate the functioning of each operation on ArrayList.

Please note that to view the results of each test, you’ll have to open the test files generated by the tool and then select “Run All”.

These generated test case can be used directly to verify the robustness of the code. If the test fails, you can examine the failing test, the failing test condition, and the test failure reason. This information can help you understand what is causing the error. Then you can either fix the error code or update the precondition of the failed test.

## Conclusion: Test-Driven Development in Visual Studio and Beyond

In conclusion, Test-Driven Development (TDD) is a great approach to quickly ensure that your code is robust and reliable. Pex is a powerful tool that complements TDD. It can automatically generate test cases so that you can identify and fix the critical issues early. Here, you have learned the concept of testing a class against various preconditioned scenarios of the operations it supports. Additionally, you have become versed with the basics of how Pex works, and how to use it effectively in the context of testing your classes. In particular, you were introduced to how Pex works, as well as how to use it to automatically generate tests and observe the outcomes of those tests. Further enhancements could involve incorporating more complex scenarios and larger software systems into testing. That being said, the process you've learned should be just as relevant today, regardless of your programming language or the size of your project — a very useful skill to have when working with any framework or programming paradigm.

